{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"*** CORRECTION TO MY PREVIOUS COMMENT: ITS CONCLUSION IS WRONG. OPTION 1 IS FEASIBLE. DO NOT ACT ON\nTHE 'OPTION 1 IS RULED OUT / OPTION 2 IS THE PATH' PARAGRAPHS ABOVE. ***\n\nMY ERROR, precisely. I tested feasibility with 'constraint-dependencies = [\"click>=8.3.3\"]'.\nconstraint-dependencies can only NARROW an existing constraint set — it cannot LIFT a cap that an\nupstream package declares. So against inspect-ai's 'click>=8.1.3,<8.2.0 | >8.2.0,<8.2.2' the\nUNSATISFIABLE result was the EXPECTED output of that tool, and it says nothing about whether the\nupgrade is achievable. I read a tool limitation as a property of the dependency graph and recorded\nthe conclusion as established. The root-cause half of that comment stands (inspect-ai is the capper;\nthe uv-version hypothesis is retracted); the REMEDIATION half does not.\n\nTHE CORRECT TOOL IS override-dependencies, WHICH REPLACES THE OFFENDING REQUIREMENT:\n\n    [tool.uv]\n    override-dependencies = [\"click>=8.3.3\"]\n\n'uv lock' -> 'Resolved 167 packages. Updated click v8.2.1 -> v8.4.2'. Fully satisfiable. Found and\nverified by the agent implementing this ticket, which also independently confirmed the root cause by\ndeleting inspect-ai from pyproject and watching click jump to 8.4.2.\n\nWHY THE OVERRIDE IS DEFENSIBLE AND NOT JUST A BIGGER HAMMER — the cap is STALE, not a real\nincompatibility:\n * inspect-ai 0.3.251 (the latest) imports cleanly under click 8.4.2, and 'inspect --help' and\n   'inspect eval --help' both render and exit 0.\n * NO inspect-ai release ever relaxes the cap, up to 0.3.251 — so waiting is not a strategy.\n * Upstream is already fixing it: UKGovernmentBEIS/inspect_ai PR #4596 against issue #4594, still\n   unmerged. That is the REMOVE-WHEN condition for the override.\n\nVERIFICATION ALREADY PASSED, and it is the part that matters:\n * 'uv lock --check' passes under BOTH uv 0.7.18 and uv 0.12.1, and the lock stays at revision 2 —\n   so it is CI-safe whichever uv 'setup-uv v9' installs. (This also disposes of my resolver-version\n   hypothesis for good.)\n * 'uv sync --locked --extra dev' resolves and installs click 8.4.2.\n * 'pip-audit --desc' over that whole environment: 'No known vulnerabilities found' — so the old pin\n   was masking nothing else, which was the specific risk I asked to have checked.\n * Diff is minimal: click 8.2.1->8.4.2, the manifest override line, and the one stale zipfile-zstd\n   marker that any relock corrects (uv 0.7.18 and 0.12.1 agree on it). The other 166 packages are\n   untouched.\n\nSO OPTION 2 IS WITHDRAWN. Suppressing PYSEC-2026-2132 would have weakened a gating security check to\nwork around a stale upstream pin that can simply be overridden — the wrong trade, and I had\npre-authorised it on a proof that did not hold. The implementer declined it and was right to.\n\nFILE-IMPACT NOTE: uv.lock stays in file_impact (I had wrongly told the implementer to drop it), and\npyproject.toml is legitimately needed too, since the lock cannot carry click >= 8.3.3 without the\noverride declaration. Both were already declared before any review ran, so nothing is staled."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785712881694136001,"uuid":"751187ef-e86c-48f7-9858-9bcfc8cf9588"}